From 5fe35bb3c0419e5b66a4685fc98c844f373e14ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:00:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] [GDN] Skip rows whose state index is NULL_BLOCK_ID

vLLM pads decode and spec-decode batches that are replayed through a
larger captured graph with rows whose state index is NULL_BLOCK_ID (0).
The Triton GDN kernels skip state indices <= 0. The XPU kernels only skip
-1, and the SSM kernels skip nothing, so padding rows load and update the
state in block 0 and write it back. In vLLM's hybrid layout block 0 is the
null page shared by every layer, and non-finite values written there turn
the attention output of the whole batch into NaN.

Treat state indices <= 0 as padding: the conv kernels compare against a
pad id of 0 instead of -1, and the decode and spec SSM kernels skip the
state load and store for such rows, as fused_recurrent does. The existing
tests drew state indices from [0, N) and now draw from [1, N); a new test
checks that padding rows leave block 0 and the real rows unchanged.

Upstream: vllm-project/vllm-xpu-kernels#660
